%I A399179 #11 Aug 31 2026 13:49:56
%S A399179 0,0,1,1,1,0,0,1,1,1,0,1,1,0,0,0,0,1,1,0,0,1,0,1,1,1,1,1,1,0
%N A399179 The sequence of binary decisions that defines the lexicographically earliest infinite path in the base-10 comma child graph.
%C A399179 We know from König's Infinity Lemma that at least one infinite path exists in the base-10 child graph (see the Angelini et al. (2024) paper for details). This path is defined by an infinite sequence of binary decisions, but at present we only know the first 30 decisions for the lexicographically earliest path. It would be nice to know more terms.
%C A399179 The analogous sequence in the base-3 case is simply 0,1,0,1,0,1,... It is conceivable that the present sequence can also be described by a finite automaton.
%H A399179 Eric Angelini, Michael S. Branicky, Giovanni Resta, N. J. A. Sloane, and David W. Wilson, The Comma Sequence: A Simple Sequence With Bizarre Properties, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.14346">arXiv:2401.14346</a>, Fibonacci Quarterly 62:3 (2024), 215-232.
%H A399179 Eric Angelini, Michael S. Branicky, Giovanni Resta, N. J. A. Sloane, and David W. Wilson, <a href="/A121805/a121805_1.pdf">The Comma Sequence: A Simple Sequence With Bizarre Properties</a>, Local copy.
%H A399179 N. J. A. Sloane, <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_EHAdf6izPI">Eric Angelini's Comma Sequence</a>, Experimental Math Seminar, Rutgers Univ., January 18, 2024, Youtube video; <a href="https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~zeilberg/expmath/sloane2024.pdf">Slides</a>
